Problem:

You are given a list of integers and a target value. Write a program to find the minimum number of operations required to transform the list into a sorted list.

Solution:

We can solve this problem using a divide-and-conquer approach. We can divide the list into two parts, and recursively apply the same operation to each part until the list is sorted.

Here's the Java code to implement the solution:
```java
public class Solution {
    public int minOperations(List<Integer> nums, int target) {
        // Base case: if the list is already sorted, return 0
        if (nums.isEmpty() || nums.get(0) <= target) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Divide the list into two parts
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.size() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            // Compare the values in the two parts
            while (left < right && nums.get(left) > nums.get(right)) {
                left++;
            }
            while (left < right && nums.get(left) < nums.get(right)) {
                right--;
            }

            // Swap the values if necessary
            if (left < right) {
                nums.swap(left, right);
            }
        }

        // Recursively apply the operation to the two parts
        return minOperations(nums.subList(left, right + 1), target);
    }
}
```
